# DBM DB2 Data and Module Scaffolding

## Wago.tools lookup rules

- Select the build/client appropriate to the requested content and pass it explicitly as `build=<build>` on every Wago DB2 lookup. The table links below identify endpoints only; add the selected build to them rather than relying on the site's default, which may select Retail even when scaffolding for another game. For example, Forever lookups should use a Forever build such as `https://wago.tools/db2/DungeonEncounter?build=1.60.1.70753&useHotfixes=1`.
- Use the latest-build index on [wago.tools](https://wago.tools/) to identify available builds for the intended game. Resolve the Classic client aliases when selecting a build: MoP is linked to Classic, Classic Titan to Wrath, Classic Anniversary to TBC, and Classic Era to Vanilla. Retail and Forever builds are listed directly.
- Beta, and PTR builds can target different game versions over time. Use the build number as a clue when identifying the game family (12 for Retail, 2 for TBC, 1.15.x for Era/Vanilla, 1.60.x for Forever, 3 for Wrath, 4 for Cataclysm, and 5 for MoP), then select a matching available build from the index. Do not select Dev, event, Venor, submission, or dark_realm builds for DB2 lookups; these channels may not have DB2 data on Wago.
- Include `?useHotfixes=1` on the table URLs below so lookups include hotfixed data. If a URL already has query parameters, append `&useHotfixes=1` instead.
- Match records by their named fields and relationships, not by a similar name alone. Check the selected build's column headers; table layouts can change. Keep encounter IDs, map IDs, journal instance IDs, journal encounter IDs, and journal section IDs distinct.

| Table | DBM use |
|-------|---------|
| [DungeonEncounter](https://wago.tools/db2/DungeonEncounter?useHotfixes=1) | Primary source for encounter names and IDs. The notable first three displayed columns are Name, encounter ID, and zone/map ID respectively. Use the encounter ID for `SetEncounterID` and resolve its zone through Map. |
| [Map](https://wago.tools/db2/Map?useHotfixes=1) | Identify zones associated with map IDs, including the zone used by `SetZone` and package map/subcategory metadata. |
| [JournalInstance](https://wago.tools/db2/JournalInstance?useHotfixes=1) | Find the Encounter Journal instance ID for a zone with a journal entry. This is the fourth argument after the colon in `DBM:NewMod`, currently named `instanceId`; it is not the map ID. |
| [JournalEncounterSection](https://wago.tools/db2/JournalEncounterSection?useHotfixes=1) | Find journal section IDs for automatic localization when no suitable spell ID exists. Follow existing negative-ID (`-sectionID`) conventions in constructors that support them, or use `DBM:EJ_GetSectionInfo(sectionID)` with the positive section ID. |
| [Difficulty](https://wago.tools/db2/Difficulty?useHotfixes=1) | Identify numeric difficulty indices and their meaning when mapping `DBM:GetCurrentInstanceDifficulty`. Reuse DBM's existing difficulty helpers rather than duplicating their mapping in a boss module. |

## Client and difficulty boundaries

- Encounter Journal support exists in Cataclysm, MoP, and Retail clients. Vanilla, TBC, Wrath, and Forever clients have no journal; omit the journal instance argument from `NewMod` there and use the package's explicit localization pattern. Do not assume a historical encounter has a journal on its original client merely because Retail has an entry for it.
- On journal-capable clients, supply the journal instance ID only for a zone that actually has a journal entry. Follow nearby modules for journal encounter-based naming versus explicit string IDs.
- A difficulty index is not a scalable level. Mythic+ keystone levels and delve tiers can require additional supported APIs; inspect existing DBM helpers and the targeted client's API contracts. Some zone hard modes instead require evidence-supported buff detection. Do not invent those checks from Difficulty table rows alone.

## Build comparisons

- Wago supports DB2 build comparisons, for example [Difficulty build diff](https://wago.tools/db2/Difficulty/diff?from=12.1.7.70493&to=12.1.7.70668). Replace the table and builds with those relevant to the task.
- Build diffs currently do **not** support hotfixes. Wago plans to add support, but do not assume it has shipped or that adding `useHotfixes=1` makes a diff hotfix-aware. Recheck support when needed; compare the individual hotfix-enabled tables if hotfixed values matter. A missing diff row does not prove a hotfixed value is unchanged.

## Scaffolding workflow

- When the user requests scaffolding from dungeon/boss names, a Wowhead article, or manually supplied data, use that information and the tables above to create the basic modules automatically. Read the supplied source and resolve metadata for the requested client; do not require the user to manually provide IDs that can be verified from those sources.
- Find the nearest minimal module in the target package and follow its naming, folder layout, `NewMod` arguments, revision marker, combat registration, and other applicable conventions. [DurgenDirgehammer](../../DBM-Dungeons/DBM-Party-Forever/HallofThanes/DurgenDirgehammer.lua) is a minimal no-journal reference, not a universal template or a source of IDs for other encounters.
- Use verified encounter and zone IDs. Add creature IDs, journal IDs, timers, warnings, aura options, or mechanics only when the supplied evidence supports them; names and DB2 metadata alone are not mechanic/timeline evidence. Do not activate guessed numeric placeholders.
- Automatically scaffold localization in the same change, without a separate prompt. Match the module's localization key and the package's existing layout; [Forever English localization](../../DBM-Dungeons/DBM-Party-Forever/localization.en.lua) shows string-keyed `GetModLocalization` and `SetGeneralLocalization` blocks. Update all applicable supported locale files using existing fallback/translation conventions; do not fabricate translated names.
- Wire the new modules into the applicable TOC/load list and update directly related package zone/subcategory metadata as needed. Preserve the client-specific TOC split and follow the [development guidance](dbm-development.instructions.md) for TOC evidence, localization, and validation.
- If an essential lookup is unavailable or multiple records remain plausible, report the specific blocker and ask for clarification rather than guessing. Otherwise complete the scaffold and its localization/load wiring from the known information. Report any intentionally unimplemented mechanics separately from completed scaffolding.

## Patch-added delve bosses and variants

- When asked to scaffold additional bosses introduced by a patch in existing delves, resolve the requested maps and query DungeonEncounter by `MapID` on the targeted build with hotfixes enabled. Example URL pattern: `https://wago.tools/db2/DungeonEncounter?filter%5BMapID%5D=230&page=1&build=<selected-build>&useHotfixes=1`. Inspect every result page, not just page 1, and repeat for each relevant map.
- Compare the returned encounter IDs against all IDs registered by existing modules in the relevant package, including every argument of multi-ID `SetEncounterID` calls and modules shared across maps. Do not infer coverage solely from filenames, boss names, or an explicit `SetZone` value.
- An uncovered encounter ID is not necessarily a new boss. Check whether it is another variant of a boss already handled by an existing module. For a confirmed shared boss variant, add the new ID to that module's existing `SetEncounterID` registration, preserving its prior IDs and behavior; do not create another module or duplicate localization/load entries.
- [SpiritflayerJinma](../../DBM-Dungeons/DBM-Delves-Midnight/Encounters/SpiritflayerJinma.lua) demonstrates one module covering three encounter IDs: `3433`, `3434`, and `3435`. This is an example of multi-ID ownership, not permission to merge unrelated bosses or assume variants have identical mechanics from a similar name alone.
- Scaffold a new module, localization, and load entry only for a genuinely distinct boss whose encounter IDs are not already covered. If variant ownership remains ambiguous, ask before merging or duplicating. Rerunning the discovery should produce no duplicate modules, encounter registrations, localization blocks, or load entries.
- Distinguish currently uncovered rows from proven patch additions. Use the requested build comparison or supplied patch evidence when attribution matters, while preserving the build-diff hotfix limitation above. Report new scaffolds, IDs added to existing modules, already-covered encounters, and unresolved matches separately.
